<p>Anthropic now offers a way to <a href="https://claude.com/resources/articles/install-as-app">convert Claude artifacts into things that look a lot like native Mac (and Windows) apps</a>.  Federico Viticci dove in and figured out how the whole system, which routes commands back through the Claude app, works, and <a href="https://www.macstories.net/stories/a-closer-look-at-claudes-mac-apps/">came to this conclusion</a>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  If I were Apple, I’d be concerned. Effectively, Anthropic shipped an app creation playground that only requires basic prompts to put together something that works. They did so by entirely bypassing the App Store or Apple’s notarization system for apps distributed outside the App Store, and by creating their own lock-in system around Claude’s identity and capabilities instead.
</p></blockquote>
<p>It&#8217;s been <a href="https://sixcolors.com/post/2026/06/road-to-wwdc-2026-whats-a-developer/">crystal clear for a while now</a> that Apple needs a simplified set of app building tools that don&#8217;t look like Xcode. This area is moving fast—even faster than I thought—and regular people are now able to <a href="https://www.theincomparable.com/doubleender/">develop their own custom apps</a> with ease.</p>
<p>Anthropic&#8217;s solution is interesting, but it&#8217;s also full of limitations and clever hacks. That&#8217;s not the issue, though. The issue is that this stuff is going to keep getting better and Apple needs to get in the game as soon as possible.</p>
